# __APP_NAME__

Run `cargo run` in this directory to start the Connect server at the address in `app.json`. In a second shell, use `node client.mjs` to read the public service setting. The response comes from the deployment configuration.

Edit the entity in `proto/app/setting.proto` and run `paydirt gen` to regenerate the standard service contract and Rust bindings. Add domain RPCs in `proto/ext/app/setting_svc.ext.proto`. Implement only the methods you override on `SettingServiceLayer` in `src/core.rs`. Other methods pass to the next service, including the config-backed Get in `src/sink.rs`. The app owns these files and assembles the layers in `src/lib.rs`. Use `paydirt gen --check` in CI.

The native server creates its tables in `paydirt.sqlite3` by default. Set `DATABASE_URL` to a PostgreSQL URL to use that backend. For a tenanted entity, provision a tenant using the local app binary and convert the response's base64 ID to a 32-character hex string and put it in the `PAYDIRT_PRINCIPALS` token-to-tenant JSON map. Only the operator with database access can invoke the binary's internal commands. The public Connect services resolve their caller from the bearer token.

`cargo run -- app.Tenant Add '{"alias":"my-team"}'` prints the new tenant with its protobuf JSON (base64) ID. App-owned entities declared in `proto/` receive a generated Diesel sink and registration in the app router. Customize a service by wrapping its typed next service in the app's `storage::Layers` implementation.

## Browser sandbox

Run `paydirt sandbox init`, install Rust's `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target, Clang and `wasm-bindgen-cli` 0.2.130. In `sandbox/`, run `npm ci`, `npm run build:wasm`, and `npm run dev`. The page calls the same app router over a MessagePort. The database Worker owns SQLite and retains rows in OPFS across page reloads.

A web client can import `openSandbox(principals)` from `sandbox/client.js`, pass `box.link.transport` to its generated Connect client, then call `box.close()` on shutdown. Supply a map of opaque bearer tokens to hex tenant IDs to provision the local sandbox. The link implementation and its Apache license come from [Payday](https://github.com/lesomnus/payday/blob/53acc135/ts/src/sandbox/link.ts).

## Entity commands

Run `paydirt entity add --watch Gauge .`, edit the resulting entity, and run `paydirt gen`. The new declaration supplies the service, table and application commands. `paydirt doctor` checks generated files and compiles the application entry points. Two consecutive additions choose different domains even before generation.

Run `cargo run -- gauge --help` for the verbs its schema declares. The lowercase commands connect to `PAYDIRT_URL` and use `PAYDIRT_TOKEN` as their bearer credential. For example, `cargo run -- gauge ls --size 10 -o json` reads one page. Requests can also be JSON arguments or read from stdin with `-`. Use `-o protojson` for protobuf JSON identifiers, `-o prototext` for protobuf text and `-o name` for an identifier per line. For example, `-o 'template={{.id}} {{.alias}}'` prints two fields. Request bodies support `--in prototext` as well as JSON. The transport closes general Patch and Apply by default. A deployment that serves them opts in with `"allow_general_writes": true` in app.json.


## App-owned behavior

Declare a domain operation in `proto/ext/`, run `paydirt gen`, then implement that method on the generated service layer and mount it in `src/lib.rs`. The request passes through Gate, Audit, your Core, and the storage Sink. Use the typed next service with the incoming request context to keep related changes in the same scoped transaction. Service overlays and application Rust sources are preserved on regeneration. A failed write rolls back the transaction, including its audit trail and outbox records.

The server reads `PAYDIRT_PRINCIPALS` when it starts. A simple tenant credential uses a JSON map such as `{"dev-token":"<32 hex digits>"}`. Set `PAYDIRT_TOKEN=dev-token` on a client to use that credential. Deployments can also supply a role policy with `PAYDIRT_POLICY_FILE` and select an account membership using the header configured by `PAYDIRT_ACCOUNT_HEADER`.

For an RPC declared with `(payday.method).account`, derive a context with `database.account_reads(ctx, "/app.InvitationService/Accept", reads)` before calling another entity's typed Get or List. Build the scope with `AccountReads::default().allow("app.Invitation", ReadFilter::AccountField("recipient".into()))`. A second declaration can select specific IDs with `ReadFilter::Ids`. Each derived context retains its own scope. Undeclared entities return an empty list or NotFound. A call served as a tenant membership uses its tenant scope.

## Native background workers

Register application workers in the `workers` function in `src/lib.rs`. Each named worker receives a stop token. Choose `Placement::Everywhere` or `Placement::Singleton`. Set `"worker_lease":"postgres"` in `app.json` to run each singleton on one PostgreSQL session across replicas. The default local lease runs workers in the current process. A fatal error shuts down the RPC listener and the other workers.

Enable scheduled schema meters with `"meter":{"directory":"measurements","every_ms":86400000,"retry_ms":60000}`. The reader starts at launch, visits tenant IDs in pages, and runs again at UTC epoch-aligned boundaries. Each file records a single tenant, meter and measurement time. Failed deliveries retry with the original readings. An app can register its own singleton with `paydirt_store::meter::periodic` and use `Pass::run_with` to supply a tenant source, custom measurements and a billing sink.

Configure the primary listener in `app.json`. Under `serving`, `tls` takes PEM certificate and key paths. Adding `client_ca` requires a verified client certificate. An optional `web` listener serves the same router at its own `listen` address with an explicit array of allowed `origins` and optional TLS. Both listeners share `deadline_ms`, `max_message_bytes` and authorization. A top-level `rate` with positive `per_second` and `burst` charges authenticated tenants one unit per RPC or per AddMany item. Set `serving.otlp_traces_endpoint` to the collector's HTTP/protobuf trace URL (including `/v1/traces`) to export RPC spans with the configured service name and W3C trace parent.

Run `cargo test --test core` to exercise the assembled router in process. The `paydirt-test` fixture gives each test its own temporary SQLite database, a fixed clock, and a repeatable sequence of domain-correct IDs. Its Connect client sends real encoded requests through the application's layers. Use `assert_code` to verify a refused call.

With entitlements enabled in the schema, configure the billing source in `app.json` as `"grant_source":{"url":"https://billing.example.org","bearer_env":"BILLING_TOKEN"}`. The native singleton calls the source's Connect GrantService. It waits for Watch readiness, consumes all List pages, applies stream updates and reconciles again on reconnect. The application continues to enforce its local grants while the source is unavailable. The optional `every_ms` and `retry_ms` settings control the reconciliation interval and initial retry delay.
